1
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:19,600
Welcome to the very first lesson on the
PMP course.

2
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:21,540
1.1 projects.

3
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:26,500
Before we start examining the PINBOC
guide, we need to stop and consider some

4
00:00:26,501 --> 00:00:31,300
basic concepts to make sure that we are
all on the same playing field.

5
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:34,980
So in this first lesson we will consider

6
00:00:34,981 --> 00:00:38,814
what is not a project
and what is a project.

7
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:42,560
So number one, what is not a project?

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,860
Organizations need to do things every day
in order to remain in business.

9
00:00:48,540 --> 00:00:52,041
These daily operations
tend to be repetitive and

10
00:00:52,042 --> 00:00:54,740
really don't take much
in the way of planning.

11
00:00:56,020 --> 00:01:00,960
Organizations usually have admission
statement which you should be able to find

12
00:01:00,961 --> 00:01:04,924
on their website and
often if you visit their office

13
00:01:04,925 --> 00:01:08,181
you'll find one in a frame
hanging on the wall somewhere.

14
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:12,460
Abmission statement says why the
organization exists.

15
00:01:12,461 --> 00:01:14,160
and what it does.

16
00:01:14,460 --> 00:01:20,500
For example, the Grey Campus mission
statement is Gray Campus transforms

17
00:01:20,501 --> 00:01:23,780
careers through skills and certification
training.

18
00:01:24,680 --> 00:01:29,660
We are a leading provider of training for
working professionals in the areas of

19
00:01:29,661 --> 00:01:33,479
project management,
big data, data science,

20
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:36,934
service management and quality management.

21
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:43,140
We offer live online, instructor led
online, classroom, instructor-led

22
00:01:43,141 --> 00:01:47,180
classroom, and e-learning, online
self-learning courses.

23
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:53,060
Our growing suite of accredited courses is
constantly upgraded to address the career

24
00:01:53,061 --> 00:01:55,740
enhancement goals of working
professionals.

25
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:59,840
And this is what Gray Campus does every
day.

26
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:04,381
We call this everyday
work operations or business

27
00:02:04,382 --> 00:02:08,240
as usual, which is often
abbreviated to BAU.

28
00:02:08,241 --> 00:02:12,327
If a student wants to start
a new course, the great

29
00:02:12,328 --> 00:02:15,401
campus staff follow a few
step-by-step instructions.

30
00:02:15,560 --> 00:02:22,380
In fact, it's so simple and repetitive
that almost all is totally automated.

31
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:28,160
There's no need to involve senior
management or a project manager or hold

32
00:02:28,161 --> 00:02:32,620
management meetings, develop a risk
management plan for a student starting a

33
00:02:32,621 --> 00:02:35,740
new course or anything out of the
ordinary.

34
00:02:35,741 --> 00:02:40,540
So then clearly operations are not
projects.

35
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:46,920
Operations maintain the organisation's
mission statement or to say another way,

36
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:50,820
operations maintain the organisation's
business as usual.

37
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:53,080
Two, what is a project?

38
00:02:54,020 --> 00:02:58,340
To understand what a project is,
we will consider grey campus again working

39
00:02:58,341 --> 00:03:01,140
every day to maintain its business as
usual.

40
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:05,040
But then something happens that is out of
the ordinary.

41
00:03:05,041 --> 00:03:08,960
For example, there could be a change to
the country's tax laws.

42
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:14,420
PMI might release a new edition of the
PIMBOC guide and Great Campus may want a

43
00:03:14,421 --> 00:03:17,220
new course developed for it, very like
this one.

44
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,420
They might decide to build a bricks and
mortar college somewhere.

45
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:30,480
They may decide to redesign their website
or redesign their office IT systems or get

46
00:03:30,481 --> 00:03:34,974
a new e-learning platform or
countless other circumstances

47
00:03:34,975 --> 00:03:39,580
that cannot be dealt with by
business as usual and that's key.

48
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:45,840
And so one of the clues that these might
be projects are each of these

49
00:03:45,841 --> 00:03:49,951
circumstances requires
a significant change

50
00:03:49,952 --> 00:03:53,500
but business as usual
does not involve change.

51
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:56,940
Now let's have a look at projects in more
detail.

52
00:03:57,540 --> 00:04:02,640
The Pinball Guide defines projects as and
please note that many of these definitions

53
00:04:02,641 --> 00:04:07,090
are critical for the
examination and obviously

54
00:04:07,091 --> 00:04:10,500
project management
etc are critical definitions.

55
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:17,160
A project is a temporary endeavour
undertaken to create a unique product,

56
00:04:17,660 --> 00:04:18,560
service or result.

57
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:21,960
Let's examine this definition a piece at a
time.

58
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:26,120
A project is a temporary endeavour.

59
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:33,060
The Oxford Dictionary defines temporary as
lasting for only a limited period of time,

60
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:34,180
not permanent.

61
00:04:34,181 --> 00:04:38,180
And so right away we can see the contrast
with operational work.

62
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:43,920
A project lasts for only a limited period
of time, or to put it another way,

63
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:48,940
a project is planned to have a start at an
end date right from the beginning.

64
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:55,980
Operational work also has a start,
but at the start, there's no specific end

65
00:04:55,981 --> 00:04:59,401
date planned, because in
reality, operational work will

66
00:04:59,402 --> 00:05:03,400
continue until it is no longer
serving a useful purpose.

67
00:05:03,401 --> 00:05:09,300
It is possible for a very large project to
last longer than some operations,

68
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:15,120
but the key point is that the project is
intended to be temporary and will

69
00:05:15,121 --> 00:05:20,140
terminate when the products, services or
results that it was designed to deliver

70
00:05:20,141 --> 00:05:22,660
have been completed and accepted by the
customer.

71
00:05:23,340 --> 00:05:28,940
Note that it says temporary endeavour,
which means it is the endeavour,

72
00:05:29,260 --> 00:05:33,300
the project, is temporary rather than the
product.

73
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:34,233
being temporary.

74
00:05:35,260 --> 00:05:38,780
Think of Australia's famous Sydney Harbour
Bridge, for example.

75
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:45,414
The project lasted eight years, but
the product has lasted 85 years so far.

76
00:05:46,180 --> 00:05:51,880
The second part of the definition is to
create a unique product, service or result.

77
00:05:52,280 --> 00:05:54,580
The word unique is a little harder to
explain.

78
00:05:55,560 --> 00:05:59,000
To be honest, I disagree with the
definition a bit.

79
00:05:59,320 --> 00:06:04,720
I think it should actually be to uniquely
create a product, service or result

80
00:06:04,721 --> 00:06:10,460
because the product itself is not
necessarily unique, it is the way the

81
00:06:10,461 --> 00:06:17,154
product is created that is unique, which
means it is the project that is unique.

82
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:21,820
But anyway, to create a unique product,
service or result, as I said earlier,

83
00:06:22,260 --> 00:06:25,305
operations are not
projects, but operations

84
00:06:25,306 --> 00:06:27,980
require a project to
initially create them.

85
00:06:27,981 --> 00:06:31,725
And to confuse matters a
little, some projects can be

86
00:06:31,726 --> 00:06:35,380
similar to operations
for part of their life cycle.

87
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:41,880
For example, suppose you're planning a
project to lay a water pipeline to the

88
00:06:41,881 --> 00:06:48,440
Sahara Desert from the source of water in
Martins in Mongolia 8,000 kilometers away.

89
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:50,460
This is clearly a project.

90
00:06:51,220 --> 00:06:55,120
But for several years it would look very
like a process.

91
00:06:55,121 --> 00:06:59,060
It should leave one piece of pipe,
next piece of pipe, next piece of pipe,

92
00:06:59,560 --> 00:06:59,980
and so on.

93
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:05,020
However, the first key difference is that
the pipeline would have a planned,

94
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:06,920
start and end it.

95
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:10,661
And the second main difference
would be that permissions

96
00:07:10,662 --> 00:07:13,540
would need to be sought
from various governments.

97
00:07:14,220 --> 00:07:19,080
Different types of terrain would present
different challenges, rocky ground,

98
00:07:20,260 --> 00:07:21,900
very soft ground and so on.

99
00:07:21,901 --> 00:07:27,520
and so it still requires risk management,
quality management, schedule management,

100
00:07:28,220 --> 00:07:31,300
cost management, resource management,
and so on.

101
00:07:31,740 --> 00:07:35,190
And it would of course require project
manager to control the project.

102
00:07:36,180 --> 00:07:38,037
How do we know what a project is finished?

103
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:43,800
Now that's an interesting point because
some of the projects that I've been

104
00:07:43,801 --> 00:07:49,480
associated with seem to just go on forever
and ever and ever end.

105
00:07:49,481 --> 00:07:53,064
The end of the project is
reached when at least one

106
00:07:53,065 --> 00:07:56,880
of the following statements
about the project is true.

107
00:07:57,360 --> 00:08:03,140
At a list like this, it's a possible
source for a question in the examination.

108
00:08:03,820 --> 00:08:05,820
So how do we know the project has ended?

109
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:08,360
Okay, so at least one of the following is
true.

110
00:08:08,700 --> 00:08:11,180
The project's objectives have been
achieved.

111
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:16,600
Number two, the objectives will not or
cannot be met.

112
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:19,460
If we can't meet them, there's no point in
the project.

113
00:08:19,480 --> 00:08:24,660
keeping the project open, wasting time and
resources, close the project.

114
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:30,120
Funding is exhausted or no longer
available for allocation to the project,

115
00:08:30,460 --> 00:08:34,220
so basically you've run out of money,
you can't afford to finish it.

116
00:08:35,040 --> 00:08:38,800
The need for the project no longer exists.

117
00:08:39,420 --> 00:08:44,460
For example, the customer no longer wants
the project completed, a change in

118
00:08:44,461 --> 00:08:48,411
strategy or priority ends
the project, the organizational

119
00:08:48,412 --> 00:08:52,500
management provides
direction to end the project.

120
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:59,280
For example, you've been working for the
last 120 years on the best steam engine

121
00:08:59,281 --> 00:09:04,000
ever, but management decides we don't
think there's going to be a huge market in

122
00:09:04,001 --> 00:09:06,860
the future for steam engines, let's just
end this project.

123
00:09:07,760 --> 00:09:12,760
Next one is the human or physical
resources are no longer available.

124
00:09:13,380 --> 00:09:18,815
Suppose you're doing a particular
project and you have specific

125
00:09:18,816 --> 00:09:22,780
Highly trained resources and
they have quit the company.

126
00:09:23,260 --> 00:09:27,480
You've looked around, you can't find
replacements in a reasonable length of time.

127
00:09:27,920 --> 00:09:30,220
It is no option but just to close your
project.

128
00:09:30,780 --> 00:09:35,320
Or the project is termed it for legal
cause or convenience.

129
00:09:36,140 --> 00:09:41,742
Your company may be wind
up or one of the contractees

130
00:09:41,743 --> 00:09:45,120
to your project by
taking legal action, etc.

131
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:49,582
Fulfilment of project
objectives may produce

132
00:09:49,583 --> 00:09:52,374
one or more of the following deliverables.

133
00:09:53,060 --> 00:09:58,400
A unique product that could be either a
component of another item, an enhancement

134
00:09:58,401 --> 00:10:02,180
or correction to an item or an item in
itself.

135
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:09,240
For example, when you're developing a new
aircraft, a project might be undertaken to

136
00:10:09,241 --> 00:10:13,280
deliver just the scope statement for the
aircraft project.

137
00:10:14,140 --> 00:10:18,920
A unique service or capability to perform
a service.

138
00:10:19,580 --> 00:10:24,120
For example, a project organisation may
wish to develop a training department.

139
00:10:25,060 --> 00:10:30,160
A unique result, such as an outcome or
document.

140
00:10:31,060 --> 00:10:36,480
Or, for example, a river flowing through a
major city has reached a non-acceptable

141
00:10:36,481 --> 00:10:40,782
level of pollution and a
project is initiated to reduce

142
00:10:40,783 --> 00:10:45,220
the pollution by 80% so
that, produces a unique result.

143
00:10:46,820 --> 00:10:51,040
A unique combination of one or more
products, services or results,

144
00:10:51,520 --> 00:10:57,374
for example new training course and
examination and a related mentoring service.

145
00:10:57,980 --> 00:11:02,840
Repetitive elements may be present in some
project deliverables and activities,

146
00:11:03,660 --> 00:11:09,160
as I mentioned before, a pipeline from
Mongolia to the Sahara Desert.

147
00:11:10,140 --> 00:11:13,105
This repetition does not
change the fundamental

148
00:11:13,106 --> 00:11:16,460
and unique characteristics
of the project work.

149
00:11:17,180 --> 00:11:22,240
Another example would be office buildings
can be constructed with the same or

150
00:11:22,241 --> 00:11:27,340
similar materials and maybe the same or
different teams.

151
00:11:27,700 --> 00:11:31,520
However, each building remains unique in
key characteristics.

152
00:11:32,180 --> 00:11:38,840
Example, location, design, environment,
location, I said location, location,

153
00:11:39,160 --> 00:11:42,100
location, that's what they always say for
buildings anyway.

154
00:11:42,101 --> 00:11:48,140
So, location, design, environment,
situation and people involved.

155
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:50,840
How many people are required for a
project?

156
00:11:51,400 --> 00:11:54,620
There's actually no minimum or maximum
number.

157
00:11:55,320 --> 00:12:00,420
A project can involve just one person or
thousands of people.

158
00:12:01,140 --> 00:12:06,374
It can involve one group in an organisation
or many groups or many organisations.

159
00:12:07,060 --> 00:12:12,640
It can exist within a single small
location such as, well, the initial

160
00:12:12,641 --> 00:12:17,540
projects for the Apple Corporation which
began in the garage of a house,

161
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:24,280
or can span many countries like modern
Apple projects, as long as the endeavor is

162
00:12:24,281 --> 00:12:29,360
intended to be temporary in nature and
will create at least one unique product,

163
00:12:29,640 --> 00:12:30,540
service or result.

164
00:12:31,060 --> 00:12:31,893
It is a project.

165
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:36,620
The Pinball Guide lists examples of
projects on page 5.

166
00:12:36,940 --> 00:12:38,840
Projects drive change.

167
00:12:39,420 --> 00:12:44,420
Processes just maintain business as usual,
They support the mission of the company,

168
00:12:45,020 --> 00:12:47,780
but for the business
to remain competitive,

169
00:12:47,781 --> 00:12:50,600
it has to occasionally
change its operations.

170
00:12:51,220 --> 00:12:55,800
It might need to add new products or
services, improve the efficiency of its

171
00:12:55,801 --> 00:13:01,160
current operations and so on, and so
projects are required, because changes

172
00:13:01,161 --> 00:13:04,440
cannot be performed under a business as
usual.

173
00:13:05,060 --> 00:13:10,140
In fact, project management has often been
called the management of change.

174
00:13:11,140 --> 00:13:14,220
Projects in the business value creation.

175
00:13:14,860 --> 00:13:19,290
PMI defines business
value as a net quantifiable

176
00:13:19,291 --> 00:13:22,021
benefit derived from a business endeavor.

177
00:13:22,900 --> 00:13:26,340
The benefit may be tangible, intangible or
both.

178
00:13:27,020 --> 00:13:33,080
In business analysis, business value is
considered the return in the form of

179
00:13:33,081 --> 00:13:35,879
elements such as time, money, goods, or

180
00:13:35,880 --> 00:13:39,220
intangibles in return for
something exchanged.

181
00:13:39,221 --> 00:13:44,339
Business value in projects
refers to the benefit that the

182
00:13:44,340 --> 00:13:48,880
results of a specific project
provides to its stakeholders.

183
00:13:49,120 --> 00:13:53,620
The benefit from projects may be tangible,
intangible or both.

184
00:13:53,960 --> 00:14:00,280
Examples of tangible elements include
monetary assets, stakeholder equity,

185
00:14:01,300 --> 00:14:04,400
utility, fixtures, tools and market share.

186
00:14:04,940 --> 00:14:11,600
Examples of intangible elements include
goodwill, brand recognition, public

187
00:14:11,601 --> 00:14:15,940
benefit, trademarks, strategic alignment
and reputation.

188
00:14:17,220 --> 00:14:18,660
Project initiation context.

189
00:14:19,420 --> 00:14:23,203
Organizational leaders initiate
projection response to four

190
00:14:23,204 --> 00:14:27,220
fundamental categories of factors
acting upon their organisations.

191
00:14:27,900 --> 00:14:30,960
As you can see in figure 1.2.

192
00:14:31,240 --> 00:14:36,660
The four categories are meet regulatory,
legal or social requirements.

193
00:14:37,240 --> 00:14:43,380
Satisfies to a order requests or needs,
implement or change business or

194
00:14:43,381 --> 00:14:46,901
technological strategies
and create improved

195
00:14:46,902 --> 00:14:50,314
or fixed products, processes or services.

196
00:14:51,080 --> 00:14:55,680
These factors influence in organisations
ongoing operations in business strategy.

197
00:14:56,400 --> 00:15:01,040
Leaders respond to these factors in order
to keep the organisation viable.

198
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:06,180
Projects provide the means for
organisations to successfully make the

199
00:15:06,181 --> 00:15:08,960
changes necessary to deal with these
factors.

200
00:15:09,480 --> 00:15:13,620
These factors ultimately should link to
the strategic objectives of the

201
00:15:13,621 --> 00:15:17,000
organisation and the business value of
each project.

